  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_d356811c8f8241b783784e29c24b5aae",
      "statement": "The output gate is the mechanism that holds write responses: Event::Output carries position Some(p) when the producing event advanced the cell's committed WAL (opening its own barrier) or None when read-only (trailing the newest open barrier, so ordinary reads pay no durability latency).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/logic/types.rs#L500-L514",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:737d6b7b775810b550a69f5b5954dbfb7987272208c113284a969d870d9524fc:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3ee0816f1070448baaae781b92583ab1",
      "statement": "Effect::AwaitDurable is a per-request durability proof that changes no cell phase, so a cell keeps serving co-resident requests while one response waits for its position to be proved durable.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/logic/types.rs#L848-L862",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:9ba4564f33d834a543088f96cd757fed871a5f8dfbfd88ab97eea36a5b045247:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_03d7f5c7b13a43139437176bb6e5cfad",
      "statement": "A bucket-proof acknowledgement reads the ownership record once and acknowledges only if it still names this node at this epoch, while a fleet proof needs no such read because the ensemble seal is its fence; this is why Event::DurableReached carries ProofSource.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/logic/types.rs#L516-L528",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:334acb2970d098534c4901b06ff1070ba90063c215aa22de0b71cbc58affe689:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/logic/types.rs#L865-L875",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8ff32cbcb86eca6ee8c143acad80cd0496ee081f3847a6bd6dd8fad44c6f7309:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_63b70906470a446da7f6ccf75ea0c018",
      "statement": "For bucket-proof acknowledgements the shell emits Effect::VerifyOwnership, reading own.json and answering whether it still names this node at this epoch before the withheld write is released.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/logic/types.rs#L865-L875",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8ff32cbcb86eca6ee8c143acad80cd0496ee081f3847a6bd6dd8fad44c6f7309:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_fa564aa695d8430daddc7452202afe8d",
      "statement": "The output gate is on by default (CELLD_OUTPUT_GATE=1); setting it to 0 removes the replication wait, with the documented consequence that the operator accepts possible loss of an acknowledged write.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/README.md#L688-L690",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c5bac6694828c6003ecce1a6cf5fa3f5220ad44682c8ecd5ee10741fe72abf94:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5dc8395f4dac40abaf3d1c6e93905f5f",
      "statement": "celld-ltx is celld's in-process SQLite replication library: it captures committed WAL data as L0 LTX segments and reports the captured position, writing segments to a filesystem or object store, restoring databases, compacting levels, creating snapshots, and reading bundle objects.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/ltx/README.md#L3-L5",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:874b14dc7097a98a8378f9c71c643e9c40a33bddd1f8373ea14afb65209da76b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_70d34b7d49f44544af09e37f672552e8",
      "statement": "The replication backend is in-process with no external replicator process or directory-watch lag: a just-written cell is registered the instant it activates, so the output gate can prove a fresh cell durable with no cold-start window.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/ltx_repl.rs#L3-L10",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e910983585e351ee55d3879c43ca7b21648d94abe50a20708c0f6d3feff8c557:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3e8aedc14b9a4589a37a7885c1127c44",
      "statement": "The LTX serialized file format is documented as Version 3 derived from superfly/ltx v0.5.2 with big-endian multi-byte integers, a 100-byte header, LZ4-block-compressed pages, a page index, and a 16-byte trailer.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/ltx/reference/ltx-format.md#L1-L21",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2ce72b483c81ef78cb4c02844cd20d084d24a984c58f4fbb9ac383cb8bf98492:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_81926c9e869a4d71ba946a346e6b1ed6",
      "statement": "Replicated data lands at cells/<cell>/ltx/e<epoch>/ in the bucket, mirroring the local <watch>/<cell>/ltx/e<epoch>/db.sqlite tree, and the backend builds its own object-store clients carrying the fleet's key prefix so two fleets sharing a bucket cannot overwrite each other.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/ltx_repl.rs#L11-L12",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ebb5d68bb74f7d2d94c329efa8e9d6e69a41905c002c7a1097db9f7a105e88b2:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/ltx/README.md#L1-L1",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b472aac1b27b9075817ad63aa1e520db0b0e7e1ef26e7f8aaf6ec4aaa9a2777e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_fd4806717f4b450e974c4548d4f01ebb",
      "statement": "Replica uploads to the epoch prefix are plain unconditional PUTs; the epoch in the key is the fence — a stale owner keeps writing but into a superseded prefix that restore does not select.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/guarantees.md#L122-L130",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c6d23ff0373609a9c34f5dbab88f29fb5c2f4559088f4a2f4436e2c1dbf34147:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_91a7cffbe0c249beb087180b81289b45",
      "statement": "A restore selects the newest epoch prefix containing LTX data and reads the full contiguous chain from transaction zero, because a node-log recovery or bundle drain can legitimately add an acknowledged tail after an earlier restore — the epoch-seal object is gone and cannot limit the chain.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/logic/restore.rs#L27-L27",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:509a225332a30014d4dce284313be1145b07798baff782603e1cb02a0b387e29:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/guarantees.md#L205-L214",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:bc3cc8e67d16059590349cb4b8aac5ff02152a162c5e35a477b1952bee385bab:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a067bef8bf564d4f8863c3fd0b0ab89d",
      "statement": "The ack rule (RPO=0): a partitioned node can commit locally and replicate into its superseded epoch prefix, but after a bucket proof the ownership record read fails to name it, so the write is not acknowledged; a paused process or a skewed clock cannot pass this because it reads the record rather than comparing clocks.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/guarantees.md#L143-L156",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:3f0e132ebf04b7d58830c93c7a2ec6c2884fdd3356fe1f930cc14446f7824aa6:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7581f79b0c56469b95a959b729625f52",
      "statement": "In fleet-durability mode (CELLD_DURABILITY=fleet, the default), each node streams per-cell L0 LTX segments it has captured but not yet uploaded to a follower ensemble; a write acknowledges when every member holds its segment on disk (write-all ack-all) or when the bucket upload proves it first — and a single-node fleet has nobody to send to, so fleet mode degenerates to bucket proofs there.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/node_log.rs#L5-L11",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2d6994cd103aff46cf6e0963027e6bdad1bc6feddfba170681bede59c9cb49ed:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/README.md#L688-L690",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c5bac6694828c6003ecce1a6cf5fa3f5220ad44682c8ecd5ee10741fe72abf94:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_c9bfb45999134010ab719023e85c54e8",
      "statement": "CELLD_DURABILITY accepts only bucket or fleet; fleet is the default so a hobbyist's first node behaves like sync-to-bucket, and the explicit opt-out remains CELLD_DURABILITY=bucket.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/main.rs#L3721-L3730",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:1f28a4c0eaa1d47778b45fe6b7c35bc61286caf1443f7a2962ed7db6b0db644f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a4c5d8e803ca493589ce1eef130828ba",
      "statement": "Node-log v0 limits are deliberate: entries travel as base64 JSON, a follower failure degrades the node to bucket-proof acks until a periodic re-recruit CASes a fresh ensemble, and recovery gathers from every reachable sealed member and requires at least one.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/node_log.rs#L19-L22",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:773b0d848d44384295e3ce669e72541f89dff7d2cf635ad5b3c88321eb282b1e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_bc1d2501858f44e8b9adf353c5617061",
      "statement": "The node-log recovery mechanism re-creates exactly the objects the dead leader would have uploaded to the bucket, so every per-cell restore and compaction mechanism stays byte-for-byte unchanged.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/node_log.rs#L9-L14",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:70b0ad7063a0464a9f3c3b7d31e7327a0925f02ff3a1e2e3544a3095b9fe8a00:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9480b7f57c0b4c60a2857b29e5c591c7",
      "statement": "Node tiering uploads are bundled for throughput: one PUT per node flush instead of per cell transaction, measured at 208x reduction (the Class A collapse), on by default with CELLD_LOG_BUNDLE=0 as the opt-out.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/celld/main.rs#L3740-L3744",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:aa8e3c2292f42d926865fce4e9018acb0c99c4ce2bd1d5fecbc0768c636fd911:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1ef320f205204553ad9d8e807d085c6f",
      "statement": "RecoverNodeLog is the takeover interlock as a core decision: the dead owner's folded log state was not sealed, so its acked tail may exist only on followers; the executor must recover every non-sealed session of the previous owner into the bucket and the activation cannot restore until NodeLogRecovered returns.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://crates/logic/types.rs#L769-L776",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:eaed267f167de899c5eda37c23b09b055997f176a91885b5bec9952f537aa1b9:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9bae8db191a64d5ea235398c5c9a8bcf",
      "statement": "A cold activation checks the prior owner's node-log record before reading the bucket: absent proves the session never acked past the bucket, sealed proves recovery completed, and open or recovering runs the recovery sequence (fence via CAS, seal reachable followers, upload their segments, then mark sealed) before restore can proceed.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/guarantees.md#L158-L175",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:19030f1b45e7fb58ae8498895e1693a9e37db598d115182f0e94bd9ed7e8c2cb:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_afd25f48872749fead5e4e41ba878f51",
      "statement": "LTX compaction and WAL hygiene are operator-tunable: CELLD_LTX_COMPACTION (default 1) enables additive L1 objects, CELLD_LTX_COMPACTION_MIN_TXIDS (default 256) delays background L1 attempts, CELLD_LTX_COMPACTIONS bounds concurrent attempts (default 2), and CELLD_LTX_TRUNCATE_PAGES (default 128 pages, 512 KiB cap) bounds the WAL the next checkpoint reads; queue cells use passive checkpoints because a truncate boundary emits a full database image.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/README.md#L693-L698",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:edbea33f00cf44e4f5ecc6161981dc7f8106f2b2f5cae96f4572082a314397ba:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_95d6eee2d7f04018a2e108eaf8dc84e4",
      "statement": "CELLD_LTX_DURABILITY_TIMEOUT_SECS defaults to 10 seconds for both the durability-proof deadline and the final snapshot retry window, and a slow or busy object store can need a longer deadline for a large write burst.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/README.md#L699-L699",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:475c5da8bda23572d0fe89fedd06c98043a5cd03aaea159cb2664332d2d0a383:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5e759106c3b346138b9ff9298882e85f",
      "statement": "Restore of an inactive cell is handled as ordinary placement work, not an emergency procedure.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/guarantees.md#L218-L223",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4c148cd4d6e40eb3a10033ceca6176b965d522e9156b69fd907a9967fd8725c8:"
        }
      ]
    }
